{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"f4b0878480da9a06","x":-1120,"y":-480,"width":1600,"height":2640,"type":"text","text":"## Reciprocal Rank fusion (RRF)\n\n\n## 🧠 What is RRF (Reciprocal Rank Fusion)?\n\n**RRF = Reciprocal Rank Fusion**, a _ranking algorithm_ that **merges multiple ranked lists of search results** — typically from different retrieval methods (like BM25 and vector search).\n\nIn plain English:\n\n> RRF gives you a way to **combine results** from multiple search systems (dense, sparse, hybrid) in a fair and effective way — **without retraining models or tuning weights**.\n\n---\n\n## ⚙️ The Intuition\n\nLet’s say you run:\n\n- A **BM25 text search** → gives you `List A` of ranked results (keyword-based)\n    \n- A **dense vector search** → gives you `List B` of ranked results (semantic-based)\n    \n\nEach list ranks the same set of documents differently.\n\nRRF merges them into **a single, final ranked list**, by giving higher weight to documents that appear _higher_ in any of the lists.\n\n---\n\n## 🔢 The Formula\n\nThe Reciprocal Rank Fusion score for a document _d_ is:\n\nRRF(d)=∑i=1N1k+ri(d)\\text{RRF}(d) = \\sum_{i=1}^{N} \\frac{1}{k + r_i(d)}RRF(d)=i=1∑N​k+ri​(d)1​\n\nWhere:\n\n- **N** = number of rankers (e.g., 2 — BM25 and dense)\n    \n- **rᵢ(d)** = rank position of document _d_ in ranker _i_ (1 = top result)\n    \n- **k** = constant (typically 60), used to control how much the top ranks dominate\n    \n\n---\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose:\n\n- BM25 ranks `doc1` as #1, `doc2` as #2\n    \n- Dense search ranks `doc2` as #1, `doc1` as #2\n    \n\nUsing **k = 60**:\n\n|Doc|BM25 Rank|Dense Rank|RRF Score|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|doc1|1|2|1/(60+1) + 1/(60+2) = **0.0328**|\n|doc2|2|1|1/(60+2) + 1/(60+1) = **0.0328**|\n\nThey get roughly equal scores — so RRF merges the lists _fairly_, giving priority to documents that are high in _either_ ranking.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Why Use RRF?\n\n### ✅ Advantages\n\n- **Simple**: No model training, just rank math.\n    \n- **Effective**: Works surprisingly well — used in academic IR competitions (TREC) and modern hybrid retrieval engines.\n    \n- **Model-agnostic**: Can fuse any ranked lists (BM25, dense embeddings, metadata rankers, etc.).\n    \n- **Prevents domination** by one retrieval method.\n    \n\n### ⚠️ Disadvantages\n\n- **Not score-aware**: Only uses _rank positions_, not raw similarity or BM25 scores.\n    \n- **Equal weighting** by default — though you can modify it to bias toward one method.\n    \n\n---\n\n## 🧩 In the Context of MongoDB Atlas (or Hybrid Search)\n\nIf you’re doing hybrid retrieval (e.g., `$vectorSearch` + `$search`), you can combine results with RRF logic in your **application layer**.\n\nExample workflow:\n\n1. Run `$search` → get top-N results (BM25)\n    \n2. Run `$vectorSearch` → get top-N results (dense)\n    \n3. Combine results client-side using the RRF formula.\n    \n4. Sort documents by their final RRF score → feed to your LLM / front-end.\n    \n\n---\n\n### Example (Python pseudocode)\n\n`def reciprocal_rank_fusion(rank_lists, k=60):     scores = {}     for results in rank_lists:         for rank, doc_id in enumerate(results, start=1):             scores[doc_id] = scores.get(doc_id, 0) + 1.0 / (k + rank)     return sorted(scores.items(), key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)  # Example usage: bm25_results = [\"doc1\", \"doc2\", \"doc3\", \"doc4\"] dense_results = [\"doc3\", \"doc1\", \"doc2\", \"doc5\"]  fused = reciprocal_rank_fusion([bm25_results, dense_results]) print(fused)`\n\nOutput (roughly):\n\n`[('doc1', 0.0328), ('doc3', 0.0328), ('doc2', 0.031), ('doc4', 0.016), ('doc5', 0.016)]`\n\nThis gives you a **final hybrid ranking** that values results found by _either_ retrieval strategy.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 In summary\n\n|Aspect|Description|\n|---|---|\n|**Full Name**|Reciprocal Rank Fusion|\n|**Purpose**|Combine multiple ranked search results into one|\n|**Formula**|Σ(1 / (k + rank))|\n|**Typical k value**|60|\n|**Used for**|Hybrid search (dense + sparse), multi-model ranking|\n|**Advantages**|Simple, robust, model-agnostic|\n|**Where it fits**|After retrieval, before final re-ranking or LLM context selection|\n\n"}
